'''venue'''<sup>1</sup>† (ven′ū), ''n.'' [Also ''venew'', ''veney'', ''venny'', ''venie''; < ME. *''venue'', ''venyw'', < OF. ''venue'', a coming, = Sp. ''venida'', arrival, attack in fencing, = It. ''venuta'', arrival, < L. ''venire'', come: see ''come''. Cf. ''venue''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' A coming.
{{smaller block|<poem>Eche of these vyve at her ''venyw''
Brougt zyx thousand as har retenyw.</poem>
{{right|''Arthur'' (ed. Furnivall), l. 307.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''old fencing'', a hit; attack; bout; a match or bout in cudgel-play; especially, a contest of regulated length, or of a fixed number of thrusts or blows; hence (because the bout was often ended when one thrust was successful), a thrust; a lunge.
{{smaller block|Three ''veneys'' for a dish of stewed prunes.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., i. 1. 296.}}
A quick ''venue'' of wit. {{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 1. 62.}}
<poem>And on his head he laies him on such load
With two quick ''vennies'' of his knotty Goad.</poem>
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas’s Weeks, ii., The Captaines.}}
<poem>Y’ have given it me,
And yet I feel life for another ''veney''.</poem>
{{right|''Chapman'', Revenge of Bussy D’Ambois, v. 1.}}
<poem>I’ve breath enough . . .
To give your perfumed worship three ''venues''.</poem>
{{right|''Middleton, Massinger, and Rowley'', Old Law, iii. 2.}}}}

